$content = <<<'HTML'
<p>Freshdesk is a practical email-first help desk, but it is not the right fit for every support operation. The main decision is whether you need deeper ticket workflows, a simpler shared inbox, customer service tied to a CRM, or a system built around messaging and AI. Start by checking the plan you use: Freshdesk and Freshdesk Omni have different channel coverage, and AI usage can affect the final bill.</p>
<h2>Quick comparison</h2>
<div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Alternative</th><th scope="col">Best fit</th><th scope="col">Starting price checked October 29, 2026</th><th scope="col">Free plan</th><th scope="col">Standout feature</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><a href="https://www.zendesk.com/pricing/">Zendesk</a></td><td>Structured ticket routing and customer context</td><td>Support Team: US$19 per agent/month billed yearly</td><td>Trial; no standard free plan listed</td><td>Routing and wider Suite plans</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://www.zoho.com/desk/pricing.html">Zoho Desk</a></td><td>Zoho users and regional pricing</td><td>India view: ₹420 per user/month billed annually; US price not confirmed</td><td>Check regional eligibility</td><td>Zoho ecosystem and multi-department workflows</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://www.helpscout.com/pricing/">Help Scout</a></td><td>Shared inbox and knowledge base</td><td>Standard: US$25 per user/month</td><td>Up to 5 users, 1 Inbox and 1 Docs site</td><td>Shared inbox and self-service</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://www.intercom.com/pricing">Intercom</a></td><td>Messaging-led support and AI</td><td>Essential: US$29 per seat/month billed yearly</td><td>No free tier confirmed in this check</td><td>Fin outcome pricing</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://www.hubspot.com/pricing/service">HubSpot Service Hub</a></td><td>CRM-connected tickets</td><td>Starter from US$7 per seat/month annual</td><td>Free tools for up to 2 users</td><td>CRM customer context</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://front.com/pricing">Front</a></td><td>Collaborative customer conversations</td><td>Starter: US$25 per seat/month billed yearly</td><td>Trial; permanent free plan not confirmed</td><td>Shared inbox and automation</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Prices and plan details can change. Each amount above links to the vendor's official pricing page. Check the plan, billing option, region, AI usage and seat rules before comparing totals.</p>
<h2>Why teams look for a Freshdesk alternative</h2>
<p>Freshdesk covers ticketing, a shared inbox, a customer portal, a knowledge base and reporting. Its current plan page also lists Freddy AI Agent sessions and paid usage items. Those are useful features, but the details matter when the team grows or adds channels.</p>
<p>The first question is which Freshdesk product you have. Freshworks describes Freshdesk as email-first, with email, Facebook and basic web chat. Freshdesk Omni supports a broader shared inbox across email, web chat, WhatsApp, Facebook and Instagram. If you need WhatsApp or Instagram, do not compare an email-first Freshdesk plan with a competitor's omnichannel plan and assume the products are equivalent. Check the official <a href="https://www.freshworks.com/freshdesk/">Freshdesk product overview</a> and <a href="https://www.freshworks.com/freshdesk/pricing/">Freshdesk pricing</a>, checked October 29, 2026.</p>
<p>Cost is another reason to compare. Freshdesk lists Growth at US$19, Pro at US$55 and Enterprise at US$89 per agent/month billed annually. The page also lists additional Freddy AI Agent sessions at US$49 per 100, Freddy AI Copilot at US$29 per agent/month on Pro and Enterprise, day passes, connector app tasks and MCP actions. A team should estimate seats and usage, not only the base plan.</p>
<p>Reporting and routing can also shape the decision. Freshdesk lists custom dashboards and intelligent routing on Pro, with skill-based routing and audit logs on Enterprise. A small team may not need those controls. A team with several queues, languages or strict service targets may need to compare the higher tiers against another vendor's plan.</p>
<p>Finally, some teams do not need a full help desk. A shared inbox may be enough for a small customer team. Others want ticket history connected to sales and CRM data, or a product-led messaging workspace. Those are different jobs, so the best alternative depends on the workflow rather than a generic ranking.</p>
<h2>1. Zendesk: for structured support operations</h2>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Teams that need ticket ownership, routing, customer context, automations and reporting across a growing support operation.</p>
<p>Zendesk is the closest like-for-like option to compare first. Its official pricing page lists Support Team at US$19 per agent/month billed yearly. Suite Team is listed at US$55 and Suite Professional at US$115 per agent/month billed yearly. The same page shows monthly billing options and paid add-ons, so compare the correct billing term and product scope. <a href="https://www.zendesk.com/pricing/">Zendesk pricing</a>, checked October 29, 2026.</p>
<p>The reason to choose Zendesk is not simply that it has more features. It is a candidate when you need to formalize assignment, customer history, reporting and escalation as the team grows. Suite plans add broader channels and knowledge capabilities.</p>
<p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> The plan ladder can be harder to compare at a glance because Support and Suite cover different jobs, while optional products and usage-based features may add cost. Do not assume every channel or AI capability is included in the entry plan.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> Your team mainly needs a lightweight shared inbox and would not use the additional routing or service-management controls.</p>
<p>Read the existing <a href="/compare/zendesk-vs-freshdesk/">Zendesk vs Freshdesk comparison</a> before switching.</p>
<h2>2. Zoho Desk: for Zoho users and price-sensitive teams</h2>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Businesses that already use Zoho CRM or want a help desk that fits a wider Zoho workflow.</p>
<p>Zoho Desk offers ticketing, web forms, workflows, a knowledge base and messaging options across its editions. Its official pricing page is regional. The page opened for this check displayed Express at ₹420 per user/month billed annually in India, followed by Standard at ₹800, Professional at ₹1,400 and Enterprise at ₹2,400. The US dollar amount was not verified in the opened view, so use the currency selector for your region rather than treating the Indian prices as US pricing. <a href="https://www.zoho.com/desk/pricing.html">Zoho Desk pricing</a>, checked October 29, 2026.</p>
<p>Zoho Desk is worth a close look when your team already uses Zoho CRM, Zoho Analytics or other Zoho products. Its value depends on whether those connections remove work from your support process. The pricing page also shows differences between editions, including messaging, custom reporting, telephony, Zia AI and multilingual help center features.</p>
<p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> The edition matrix needs careful review. Some features that look like core help desk functions may sit on a higher tier, and regional pricing makes a direct comparison harder if you are buying in another market.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> Your team is committed to a different CRM ecosystem or needs a channel and routing combination that the chosen Zoho edition does not include.</p>
<h2>3. Help Scout: for a shared inbox and self-service</h2>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Smaller teams that want customer conversations to feel close to email, with a knowledge base alongside the inbox.</p>
<p>Help Scout's pricing page lists a free plan for up to five users, one inbox and one Docs site. Its Standard plan was displayed at US$25 per user/month when checked; Plus was US$45 and Pro US$75 per user/month. The page also lists AI Answers as a separate usage-based option at US$0.75 per resolution. <a href="https://www.helpscout.com/pricing/">Help Scout pricing</a>, checked October 29, 2026.</p>
<p>Help Scout is a sensible alternative when the main problem is team collaboration around customer email, not a need for a large service operation. Its inbox, saved replies, workflows and Docs knowledge base can cover common support work without asking a team to adopt a more complex ticketing model than it needs.</p>
<p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> Teams that depend on advanced routing, complex service-level policies or a wide set of contact-center channels should compare the exact plan requirements. The free plan is also limited in inbox and Docs site count.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> Your support model depends on intricate multi-channel rules or enterprise governance controls that are not in your selected plan.</p>
<h2>4. Intercom: for messaging-led support and AI resolution</h2>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> SaaS and digital products where customers contact support through in-app chat, messaging and a product experience.</p>
<p>Intercom's official pricing page lists Essential at US$29 per seat/month, Advanced at US$85 and Expert at US$132 when billed annually. Fin AI Agent is priced from US$0.99 per outcome. Intercom explains that the bill has two parts: seat charges and usage for Fin or certain messaging channels. <a href="https://www.intercom.com/pricing">Intercom pricing</a>, checked October 29, 2026.</p>
<p>This model can fit a team that wants to connect support to in-app messaging and automate defined customer requests. It is a different choice from a traditional email-first ticket queue because customer conversations, help content and AI resolution are central to the workflow.</p>
<p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> Outcome-based AI pricing needs a volume estimate. Model the number of conversations that may count as a Fin outcome, and review separate channel charges before comparing Intercom with a per-agent help desk.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> Most requests arrive through email and the team does not need messaging-led customer engagement.</p>
<h2>5. HubSpot Service Hub: for CRM-connected support</h2>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Teams that want support tickets, customer records and sales context to live in the same CRM system.</p>
<p>HubSpot offers free service tools for up to two users. Its Service Hub pricing page displayed Starter from US$7 per seat/month on annual billing, alongside higher tiers with more service features. Professional and Enterprise have required one-time onboarding fees shown on the page, and HubSpot Credits can add usage costs. <a href="https://www.hubspot.com/pricing/service">HubSpot Service Hub pricing</a>, checked October 29, 2026.</p>
<p>HubSpot is worth evaluating when agents need a clear view of the customer's CRM history and the business wants support activity connected to sales and retention work. It may reduce the need to move between separate CRM and help desk systems.</p>
<p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> Do not compare only the Starter price. Seat types, onboarding, credits and higher-tier requirements can change the total cost. A business that only needs a standalone help desk may not benefit from buying into a broader CRM platform.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> Your team does not use HubSpot and does not need its CRM context enough to justify adopting more of the platform.</p>
<h2>6. Front: for collaborative customer conversations</h2>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Teams that handle customer conversations together and want shared inboxes, ticketing, rules and reporting in one workspace.</p>
<p>Front's official pricing page lists Starter at US$25 per seat/month, Professional at US$65 and Enterprise at US$105 when billed annually. Starter focuses on single-channel support with a shared inbox and ticketing; Professional adds omnichannel support, more automation and advanced analytics. Enterprise adds further controls and AI features. <a href="https://front.com/pricing">Front pricing</a>, checked October 29, 2026.</p>
<p>Front is useful when the team sees customer support as collaborative communication across several groups rather than only a ticket queue. Shared inboxes, assignment and internal collaboration can suit teams that need sales, operations and support to coordinate on a customer conversation.</p>
<p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> The seat price rises with the plan, and AI capabilities may be add-ons on lower tiers. Check channel needs, seat limits and automation rules before you treat Starter as equivalent to a higher Freshdesk plan.</p>
<p><strong>Skip it if:</strong> You need a very low-cost entry for many agents or want a usage-based model tied to ticket volume instead of seats.</p>
<h2>How to choose without creating a migration problem</h2>
<p>Start with a list of the tasks your current setup handles. Include channels, ticket fields, groups, assignment rules, business hours, service targets, saved responses, knowledge articles, reports and integrations. Mark each item as essential, useful or unused. This prevents a new vendor's longer feature list from distracting you from the workflows your team depends on.</p>
<p>Then estimate total cost for a realistic month. Count paid agents, expected AI usage, any messaging or voice add-ons, onboarding, and overage charges. Compare equivalent plans. A lower per-agent price can still cost more if the feature you need is sold separately; a ticket-volume model can become expensive when reply volume spikes.</p>
<p>Plan the export before cancelling Freshdesk. Freshdesk's current support guide says ticket-list exports can be downloaded as CSV or Excel and filtered by date, agent, group, status and other fields. It also warns that this export does not include the entire conversation or archived tickets. A separate account export is available to an account administrator. Read the <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/225158">ticket export guide</a> and <a href="https://support.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/225487-how-do-i-export-my-helpdesk-data-">account export guide</a>, both checked October 29, 2026.</p>
<p>Do not assume a destination product will import every field or keep historical reports. Ask the new vendor what it accepts, map the exported fields, test a small sample and compare record counts. Rebuild rules and reports deliberately. Keep the old account available until agents can find the records they need and the new workflow has been checked.</p>
<h2>Common questions</h2>
<h3>Which Freshdesk alternative should I compare first?</h3>
<p>Start with Zendesk for a like-for-like ticketing comparison. Compare Help Scout if you want a simpler shared inbox, Intercom for messaging and AI outcomes, HubSpot for CRM-connected support, Front for collaborative conversations, or Zoho Desk if its regional pricing and ecosystem fit your business.</p>
<h3>Is Freshdesk cheaper than Zendesk?</h3>
<p>Their published entry annual prices were both US$19 per agent/month on October 29, 2026: Freshdesk Growth and Zendesk Support Team. That does not make the plans identical. Compare the channels, automation, reporting, AI usage and add-ons you will actually use.</p>
<h3>Does Freshdesk have a free plan?</h3>
<p>The standard Freshdesk pricing page displayed Growth, Pro and Enterprise, and a 14-day free trial. Freshworks also publishes information about a Free program, but it was not presented as a standard self-serve plan on the pricing page checked. Ask Freshworks whether your account qualifies before budgeting around it.</p>
<h3>Can I export all Freshdesk ticket data?</h3>
<p>Freshdesk documents ticket-list exports in CSV or Excel, but the filtered ticket export does not include the entire conversation or archived tickets. An account administrator can request an account export. Confirm the export type and permissions for your account before you migrate.</p>
<h3>Should I choose a help desk or a shared inbox?</h3>
<p>Choose a help desk when ownership, ticket status, service targets, routing and reporting are central. A shared inbox may be enough when a small team mainly needs to coordinate email and publish a knowledge base. Map the actual work before choosing.</p>
